// pescDerivedEval.recipe.jsonc — LUNAR_PRISM, 2026-08-17.
//
// PESC and the CEDS hub ONLY, plus the PESC -> CEDS derived bridge over the PROPERTY TIER. This is the
// cheap evaluation build: P2's debug census runs here, not on the four-forge eye graph, because a census
// is a statement about the PESC/CEDS pairing and the other two standards contribute nothing to it while
// costing a forge each.
//
// ============================ ONE BRIDGE PER RECIPE — RULING P2-R5 ============================
// ⚠️ THIS RECIPE PREVIOUSLY DECLARED BOTH PESC PLUGINS AND COULD NOT BE BUILT. The option-set tier now
// lives in its own sibling recipe, `pescOptionSetDerivedEval.recipe.jsonc`. THIS IS NOT A RETREAT FROM
// THE TWO-PLUGIN DESIGN — both plugins are shipped, validated and censused; they simply cannot share a
// MANIFEST.
//
// THE CODE FACT, so nobody re-attempts it. [code fact] vocabulary.js:183-198 composes a relationship
// subject from exactly five inputs — hubStandard, hubVersion, sourceStandard, sourceVersion, producer.
// THE PLUGIN IS NOT AMONG THEM. [code fact] build.js:1740-1745 derives producer 'inferred' for any block
// carrying a decisionBlock, and both plugins are frozen-judge derived plugins. Both therefore compose
// the IDENTICAL subject `ceds@<ver>_rel_pesc260805@<ver>_close`, and manifestEditor refuses the second:
// "One subject contributes one schema block of one kind." THE FRAMEWORK IS RIGHT. A manifest keyed on
// schemaBlockRefId + position cannot hold two blocks claiming one subject without the second being
// ambiguous. This is arithmetic over the composer's inputs, not a defect in either declaration.
//
// WHY NOBODY MET THIS BEFORE: every other recipe in this repository declares exactly ONE bridge. The two
// PESC recipes were the first two-bridge recipes that had ever existed here.
//
// ⚠️ A REFUSAL THAT ARRIVES TOO LATE — docketed as framework item 16, NOT repaired here. The two-bridge
// form VALIDATED CLEAN and was refused only in phase C, AFTER both censuses had run, both SSSOM exports
// were written and both blocks were frozen. On a real-judge run that ordering would spend the entire
// batch and THEN refuse. Single-bridge recipes cannot trip a two-bridge refusal, so the hazard is
// DISARMED for this order — docketed, not live.
//
// A PATH CONSIDERED AND REFUSED, recorded so it reads as seen rather than missed: declaring the
// option-set plugin's producer as 'structural' would compose a distinct subject (`_struct`) and would
// work mechanically. It would also be a lie — that plugin is an inferred judge, not a same-URI
// structural join — and the producer suffix exists precisely so a block cannot misdescribe its own
// provenance. Using it as a name-uniquifier defeats the mechanism it borrows.
//
// ============================ THE TWO POPULATIONS ARE NEVER POOLED ============================
// Standing reporting rule, and it has bitten this order in three different forms. Two populations
// embedded on differently-shaped strings are not mutually comparable: the element-tier subjects carry
// the ruled HYBRID composition; the option-set subjects carry `Artifact vN | TypeName`, which
// REPORT-P0 §4.1 called pure noise and which the composition test measured as the losing arm. Any
// pooled mean across the two is a meaningless number that will read as a measurement.
// THE SPLIT MAKES THIS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN DISCIPLINARY — two runs cannot produce a pooled figure by
// accident. What is LOST is the single-run guarantee against a stale cross-run comparison, so any report
// citing both tiers MUST name the run each figure came from.
//
// ROUND-TRIP STAGE ON. A bridge writes edges, never nodes, so the PESC round trip must remain
// numerically identical to its pin with the bridge present. It is cheap and it is the assertion that
// the bridge did not disturb the standard's own bytes.
//
// SPEND: the DEBUG judge costs nothing. A REAL-judge line is authorised PER LINE, as data, in
// acceptanceCommands.jsonc — never by editing this file.
{
	"schemaVersion": "1.0.0",
	"recipeName": "pescDerivedEval",
	"kind": "dev",
	"description": "PESC and the CEDS hub only, PLUS the PESC -> CEDS DERIVED bridge over the PROPERTY TIER (pescCedsDerivedPlugin over PescElementDecl) — the cheap evaluation build for P2. The option-set tier is a SEPARATE recipe, pescOptionSetDerivedEval, per RULING P2-R5: two derived plugins over one standard pair compose an identical manifest subject and cannot share a manifest.",
	"standards": [
		{ "token": "ceds", "version": "current" },
		{ "token": "pesc260805", "version": "current" }
	],
	"hubs": [
		{ "standard": "ceds" }
	],
	"bridges": [
		{ "source": "pesc260805", "hub": "ceds", "bridge": "pescCedsDerivedPlugin", "dependencies": ["pesc260805", "ceds"] }
	],
	"roundTripStage": true
}
